::Q1::What is the main purpose of using a mock in a unit test?
{
~To execute the real external dependency faster
=To replace a real dependency and control and record how it is used
~To test the dependency's internal implementation
~To permanently replace the dependency in the application
}

::Q2::Which problem can occur when a unit test makes a real network call?
{
~The test becomes independent of external systems
=The test can become slow or unreliable when the external service is unavailable
~The test automatically gains better code coverage
~The test no longer requires assertions
}

::Q3::A module named mymodule imports requests and calls requests.get. Which target should normally be patched?
{
~requests.get where it was originally defined
=mymodule.requests.get where the module under test looks it up
~pytest.requests.get
~unittest.requests.get
}

::Q4::Which Mock feature is appropriate for simulating a ConnectionError during a test?
{
~return_value
~call_count
=side_effect
~call_args
}

::Q5::When is mocking generally most appropriate?
{
~When testing the function being mocked
~When a dependency is cheap, reliable, and easy to use directly
=When a dependency is genuinely external, slow, or unreliable
~When every operation in the test should be mocked
}